; bfsodium ChaCha20_Poly1305 AEAD  RFC 8439 section 2 point 8
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; The block function that keeps its key  the exclusive
; or  the adder that steps the block counter  the r clamp  the Poly1305 absorb
; step and the wide adder that finishes the tag are each PASTED from their own
; verified files  so this file is the plumbing that joins them and nothing else;
;
; IO  in:  key{32}  nonce{12}  alen{2} LE  aad{alen}  plen{2} LE  pt{plen}
;     out: ciphertext{plen}  tag{16}
;
; Both lengths are read before the bytes they count  and a bfsodium primitive
; never relies on end of input;
;
; The ciphertext is never stored; sixteen bytes are encrypted  written out and
; folded into the authenticator  and then the next sixteen; So the tape does
; not grow with the message  which is the point: the AEAD that was never
; committed reached 903k lines by unrolling over the message instead;
;
; The padded message the tag is taken over is
;   AAD  pad16  ciphertext  pad16  len(AAD){8} LE  len(C){8} LE
; and EVERY piece of that is padded to a multiple of sixteen  so every block is
; full and the ONE that ends a block always sits at blk{16}; None of the flag
; driven part block machinery poly1305 needs is needed here; padding is landing
; a nought when the input has run out;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x02f  saved{48}  key{32} @0x000  ctr{4} @0x020  nonce{12} @0x024;
;                 blockkeep wants them at ITS base and hands them back intact
;   @0x040:0x14f  blockkeep's frame; it runs HERE at this file's own zero and
;                 its footprint 0:335 is what sets the size of it; a keystream
;                 block comes back at @0x040:0x07f
;   @0x0d0:0x0f7  the tag frame; add136 runs at @0x0d0 for the one addition
;                 this file makes itself  the s at the end; it sits above the
;                 keystream  in tape the block function has already given back
;   @0x154:0x155  alen{2} u16 LE  kept  because the length block needs it after
;                 the count has been run down to nought
;   @0x156:0x157  plen{2} u16 LE  kept  for the same reason; BOTH sit above
;                 blockkeep's frame  not below it: cells 0x030 upward are
;                 inside that footprint  and blockkeep asserts them clear on
;                 entry  so a length parked there is caught on the second block
;   @0x160:0x179  the exclusive or frame; xor32 footprint 0:25 at @0x160
;   @0x180:0x18d  the adder frame; add32 footprint 0:13 at @0x180
;   @0x190:0x196  the AAD count frame
;   @0x198:0x19e  the plaintext count frame; the two frames have the SAME shape
;                 inside  which is what lets one piece of counting code serve
;                 both: rem{2} at plus 0  nz at plus 2  a spare at plus 3  the
;                 else arm at plus 4  and a copy and its spare at plus 5 and 6
;   @0x1a0        k     sixteen rounds  one per byte position of a block
;   @0x1a1        j     keystream bytes left in this block
;   @0x1a2        t     a copy  so j can be tested without being eaten
;   @0x1a3        u     puts that copy back
;   @0x1a4        f     set while the keystream is spent
;   @0x1b0:0x1c0  r{17}    the clamped r  copied into absorb's slot each block
;   @0x1c4:0x1d4  s{17}    added in once at the very end
;   @0x1d8:0x1e8  tmp{17}  hands r back  and hands a length back
;   @0x200:0x32f  the absorb frame; poly1305/absorb runs HERE and its footprint
;                 0:303 sets the size of it; absorb takes r at @0x200  and the
;                 accumulator at @0x211 and the block at @0x222 LIVE there
;                 between calls  so only r is handed in afresh each time;
;                 clamp borrows the head of this frame once  before any block
;
; The accumulator and the block live inside absorb's own slots rather than
; being carried in and out  so the glue for one block is the copy of r and
; nothing else; absorb consumes r  which is why a master copy is kept apart;
;
; A byte always lands on the TOP of the block  at blk{16}  and the block then
; slides down one; a byte taken in round k is shifted sixteen minus k more
; times and comes to rest at blk{k}  so the write needs no index; When the
; input has run out no byte lands  and the nought that was there rides down
; instead  which is exactly pad16 and costs nothing;
;
; saved sits at the bottom because blockkeep wants it at its own base  and
; because a read prologue may hold only reads and single steps: a long run of
; steps before the first read is split across lines by the layout pass and a
; paste begins at the prologue  so those steps would be dropped;

; read the key  step over the counter  then read the nonce
  ,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>>>>>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
; ASSERT ptr=47
; ASSERT zero 48:815

